===Noun===
{{en-noun}}
{{listen|Laughter.ogg|Recorded laughter of a woman}}

# An expression of [[mirth]] particular to the human [[species]]; the sound heard in laughing; [[laughter]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1803|author=w:Oliver Goldsmith|title=The Poetical Works of Oliver Goldsmith, M.B.: With an Account of His Life|page=45
|passage=And the loud '''laugh''' that spoke the vacant mind.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1869|author=w:F. W. Robertson|title=Lectures and Addresses on Literary and Social Topics|page=87
|passage=That man is a bad man who has not within him the power of a hearty '''laugh'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|His deep '''laughs''' boomed through the room.}}
# Something that provokes [[mirth]] or [[scorn]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1921|author=w:Ring Lardner|title=The Big Town: How I and the Mrs. Go to New York to See Life and Get Katie a Husband|publisher=The Bobbs-Merrill Company|page=73
|passage=“And this rug,” he says, stomping on an old rag carpet. “How much do you suppose that cost?” ¶ It was my first guess, so I said fifty dollars. ¶ “'''That’s a laugh''',” he said. “I paid two thousand for that rug.”}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author=w:Monty Python|title=w:Always Look on the Bright Side of Life
|passage=Life's a piece of shit / When you look at it / Life's a '''laugh''' and death's a joke, it's true.}}
#: {{ux|en|Your new hat's an absolute '''laugh''', dude.}}
# {{lb|en|Britain|NZ}} A [[fun]] person.
#* '''2010''', {{w|The Times}}, March 14, 2010, ''{{w|Tamzin Outhwaite}}, the unlikely musical star''
#*: Outhwaite is a good '''laugh''', yes, she knows how to smile: but deep down, she really is strong and stern.

